00:01:12.555 --> 00:01:14.625
"Homeroom with Sal The Podcast",

00:01:14.625 --> 00:01:17.365
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:20.515
With that, I am excited
to introduce our guest,

00:01:20.515 --> 00:01:24.275
Bob Hughes, director of US K-12 Education

00:01:24.275 --> 00:01:26.355
at the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation.

00:01:26.355 --> 00:01:28.475
Bob, welcome.

00:01:28.475 --> 00:01:30.565
- Sal it's great to be here.

00:01:30.565 --> 00:01:33.205
I have to tell you, I talked
to my dad about you last night,

00:01:33.205 --> 00:01:34.445
and he said thank him,

00:01:34.445 --> 00:01:36.668
I learned calculus on the Academy website.

00:01:37.875 --> 00:01:39.995
- Oh, was your dad
learning calculus for fun,

00:01:39.995 --> 00:01:43.185
or for pragmatic reasons?

00:01:43.185 --> 00:01:44.018
- For fun.

00:01:44.018 --> 00:01:46.265
He's 85, so he really enjoyed it

00:01:46.265 --> 00:01:48.625
and it actually dipped into
the humanities as well.

00:01:48.625 --> 00:01:52.985
So you're a family favorite,
it's great to be here.

00:01:52.985 --> 00:01:56.125
- No, that's always
good to hear especially

00:01:56.125 --> 00:01:57.725
when people are learning
math for math's sake.

00:01:57.725 --> 00:01:59.555
Although, there's no shame in learning it

00:01:59.555 --> 00:02:01.075
for pragmatic reasons.

00:02:01.075 --> 00:02:03.725
So Bob, we've known
each other for a while,

00:02:03.725 --> 00:02:05.975
but I always have fun going
to these conversations

00:02:05.975 --> 00:02:09.275
cause we can go deeper
than we normally go.

00:02:09.275 --> 00:02:12.155
But I will remind everyone
watching on Facebook and YouTube

00:02:12.155 --> 00:02:14.705
if you have any questions
for Bob or myself

00:02:14.705 --> 00:02:18.435
about foundations, about
math, about education

00:02:18.435 --> 00:02:22.035
about really anything about Bob's life,

00:02:22.035 --> 00:02:24.895
feel free to put those
questions in the chat.

00:02:24.895 --> 00:02:26.655
We have team members who
will surface it to us,

00:02:26.655 --> 00:02:29.255
and I'll try to get to as
many of those questions

00:02:29.255 --> 00:02:30.845
as possible.

00:02:30.845 --> 00:02:32.385
But Bob and I have to get full disclaimer,

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.218
to the Gates Foundation.

00:02:33.218 --> 00:02:34.815
I think many folks know,

00:02:34.815 --> 00:02:38.345
is one of the largest
funders of Khan Academy.

00:02:38.345 --> 00:02:41.151
So I definitely have a bias
going into this interview.

00:02:41.151 --> 00:02:43.379
(laughs)

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:44.308
I will just call out.

00:02:45.935 --> 00:02:46.768
So Bob,

00:02:48.205 --> 00:02:50.415
- We've signed the grant agreements.

00:02:50.415 --> 00:02:52.405
- Yeah, I think it said
like no hard questions

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.042
in the grant agreement.

00:02:55.339 --> 00:02:56.639
In fact, I was like, sure,

00:02:57.705 --> 00:02:59.095
I'm gonna ask you a few hard ones.

00:02:59.095 --> 00:03:02.995
But maybe a good place to start Bob is,

00:03:02.995 --> 00:03:06.485
what does the director of U K-12 education

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:08.525
at the Bill and Melinda
Gates foundation do?

00:03:08.525 --> 00:03:09.405
What is your focus?

00:03:09.405 --> 00:03:11.378
What do you hope to do in the world?

00:03:12.305 --> 00:03:13.545
- So we've been working with Bill

00:03:13.545 --> 00:03:15.505
and Melinda for, I've
been working with Bill

00:03:15.505 --> 00:03:18.055
and Melinda for five years
to really think about,

00:03:18.055 --> 00:03:19.955
how we can take knowledge.

00:03:19.955 --> 00:03:21.735
Good research based knowledge and turn it

00:03:21.735 --> 00:03:24.365
into practices that can benefit more kids.

00:03:24.365 --> 00:03:26.575
And specifically we
shifted our strategy about

00:03:26.575 --> 00:03:28.335
five years ago, to really focus

00:03:28.335 --> 00:03:31.585
on black and Latino youth and
youth experiencing poverty.

00:03:31.585 --> 00:03:33.345
When we looked at the
data, the United State

00:03:33.345 --> 00:03:35.835
you may remember in the old
days waiting for Superman

00:03:35.835 --> 00:03:37.605
when we were talking a lot about

00:03:37.605 --> 00:03:40.155
how the United States
with 15th in the world.

00:03:40.155 --> 00:03:42.195
When we looked at that
data and we desegregated

00:03:42.195 --> 00:03:46.285
by race, and we consistently
saw that white students were

00:03:46.285 --> 00:03:48.225
in the top two or three
nations in the world

00:03:48.225 --> 00:03:50.175
on tests like the PISA exam.

00:03:50.175 --> 00:03:52.015
And African American and Latino students

00:03:52.015 --> 00:03:55.295
were way down at the bottom
of the OECD countries

00:03:55.295 --> 00:03:56.625
who were actually taking the exams.

00:03:56.625 --> 00:03:59.715
We really thought, just as
a matter or a mathematics,

00:03:59.715 --> 00:04:02.495
how are we gonna improve
a US public education?

00:04:02.495 --> 00:04:04.655
We really decided to shift our focus

00:04:04.655 --> 00:04:07.025
to black and Latino, young people

00:04:07.025 --> 00:04:09.595
and students experiencing
poverty of all colors.

00:04:09.595 --> 00:04:12.605
And so, we believe that the
strategies we're pursuing

00:04:12.605 --> 00:04:14.715
and I can tell you some of
them will help all kids,

00:04:14.715 --> 00:04:17.865
but we're really focused on
those target populations.

00:04:17.865 --> 00:04:19.635
Things we're doing are
right up your alley.

00:04:19.635 --> 00:04:22.025
One of the reasons we support
you Sal, we deeply believe

00:04:22.025 --> 00:04:25.365
in curriculum and comprehensive
instructional systems.

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.955
The goal being that you give
students opportunities, both

00:04:27.955 --> 00:04:30.385
inside the classroom and
outside the classroom.

00:04:30.385 --> 00:04:31.995
To master those skills,

00:04:31.995 --> 00:04:34.635
that are so essential to adult success.

00:04:34.635 --> 00:04:37.255
We put a lot of energy in
building full course curriculum

00:04:37.255 --> 00:04:40.265
over the last five years, and
then trying to scale those

00:04:40.265 --> 00:04:43.255
with districts and intermediaries
across the country.

00:04:43.255 --> 00:04:45.365
A second thing we're really
focused on is teacher prep.

00:04:45.365 --> 00:04:47.565
Like we need great teachers.

00:04:47.565 --> 00:04:49.105
We're lucky to have so many good ones,

00:04:49.105 --> 00:04:50.835
but we need so many more.

00:04:50.835 --> 00:04:51.865
We're really hunkered down

00:04:51.865 --> 00:04:54.975
on this teacher residency model
where you prepare a teacher,

00:04:54.975 --> 00:04:57.965
much like you would
prepare a doctor or a nurse

00:04:57.965 --> 00:04:59.475
where you put them in a
high-performing school

00:04:59.475 --> 00:05:01.315
and give them real support.

00:05:01.315 --> 00:05:03.085
And then a final, big area.

00:05:03.085 --> 00:05:04.315
There are a couple of
others, but I'll just

00:05:04.315 --> 00:05:05.745
stick with some of the headlines.

00:05:05.745 --> 00:05:07.855
Is something we call
Continuous Improvement Networks

00:05:07.855 --> 00:05:09.455
For School Improvement.

00:05:09.455 --> 00:05:11.275
In 24 cities across the country,

00:05:11.275 --> 00:05:13.855
we've been funding
intermediaries to really focus

00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:17.395
on one of three big problems,
getting kids on track.

00:05:17.395 --> 00:05:19.025
And by the end of eighth grade,

00:05:19.025 --> 00:05:20.605
that ninth grade transition year.

00:05:20.605 --> 00:05:22.175
Which is so important and predictive

00:05:22.175 --> 00:05:24.765
of adult success and
ultimately transitions

00:05:24.765 --> 00:05:27.265
between high school and a
post-secondary experience.

00:05:27.265 --> 00:05:29.009
Like a two or four year community college.

00:05:29.009 --> 00:05:30.825
We're really hunkered down on those.

00:05:30.825 --> 00:05:32.305
We've asked groups to come up

00:05:32.305 --> 00:05:35.095
with innovative strategies
based on research and data.

00:05:35.095 --> 00:05:38.315
They can implement in their communities.

00:05:38.315 --> 00:05:39.815
Then we're slowly tracking those

00:05:39.815 --> 00:05:41.961
and trying to get good
lessons from the field and

00:05:41.961 --> 00:05:44.178
and spread those across the country.

00:05:45.485 --> 00:05:49.465
- And what's your sense of
either cynicism or optimism?

00:05:49.465 --> 00:05:52.155
when I talked to a lot of few
folks in the education world

00:05:52.155 --> 00:05:53.695
which we're both in.

00:05:53.695 --> 00:05:55.715
There's people are excited
about certain things,

00:05:55.715 --> 00:05:57.185
but they're like, yeah,

00:05:57.185 --> 00:06:00.885
decades of smart people
trying really good ideas.

00:06:00.885 --> 00:06:04.225
But it seems to not make
there, have been measurable

00:06:04.225 --> 00:06:05.225
positive impacts.

00:06:05.225 --> 00:06:08.438
But when you look about
the whole arc of things,

00:06:08.438 --> 00:06:10.375
peop there's at least not
a feeling that there's

00:06:10.375 --> 00:06:13.775
been a dramatic improvement
over the last several decades.

00:06:13.775 --> 00:06:16.085
What's your sense if that
is true, what's your sense

00:06:16.085 --> 00:06:19.155
of why it's been so difficult
and what gives you hope,

00:06:19.155 --> 00:06:22.198
that the next 20 or 30 or
40 years could be different?

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:25.435
- I think we've put some really important

00:06:25.435 --> 00:06:27.525
pieces of the puzzle in place,

00:06:27.525 --> 00:06:29.225
that will enable us to make real progress

00:06:29.225 --> 00:06:31.415
over the next 10 or 15 years.

00:06:31.415 --> 00:06:34.055
A concrete example is the
common core standards.

00:06:34.055 --> 00:06:36.915
Those were really
powerful and aligning lots

00:06:36.915 --> 00:06:39.185
of people along a common
framework, to really

00:06:39.185 --> 00:06:41.465
help folks understand what works

00:06:41.465 --> 00:06:43.078
and what should be not what works.

00:06:43.078 --> 00:06:44.935
But what should it be
mastered by students?

00:06:44.935 --> 00:06:46.915
What should they know and be able to do

00:06:46.915 --> 00:06:49.285
along a developmental progression?

00:06:49.285 --> 00:06:51.945
Now, what we're doing is the
second stage of that work,

00:06:51.945 --> 00:06:53.955
which is really thinking about curriculum.

00:06:53.955 --> 00:06:56.515
Thinking about SEL, thinking
about all the supports

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:57.865
that need to be in place.

00:06:57.865 --> 00:06:59.625
To make the promises, those standards

00:06:59.625 --> 00:07:01.975
of reality for young
people in their schools.

00:07:01.975 --> 00:07:03.905
So, we're starting to see bright spots

00:07:03.905 --> 00:07:06.035
in lots of different areas.

00:07:06.035 --> 00:07:09.105
Urban areas frequently are criticized

00:07:09.105 --> 00:07:11.885
as being cesspools of
ineffective practice.

00:07:11.885 --> 00:07:14.355
But in fact, you see
in places like New York

00:07:14.355 --> 00:07:16.145
and even in Los Angeles and a variety

00:07:16.145 --> 00:07:20.145
of urban areas, increases in
graduation rates slow increases

00:07:20.145 --> 00:07:23.575
in standardized test scores,
slow increases in preparation

00:07:23.575 --> 00:07:25.655
for career in college
as measured in New York.

00:07:25.655 --> 00:07:29.055
For example, by completion
of the advanced Regents exam,

00:07:29.055 --> 00:07:30.285
all of those things are good.

00:07:30.285 --> 00:07:33.375
So yes, we haven't had a tidal wave

00:07:33.375 --> 00:07:34.925
or a giant transformation.

00:07:34.925 --> 00:07:36.745
But I think every single day

00:07:36.745 --> 00:07:38.495
we're making small steps that ensure,

00:07:38.495 --> 00:07:41.585
that more kids will be
able to complete standards

00:07:41.585 --> 00:07:43.075
and go on to a productive life.

00:07:43.075 --> 00:07:45.645
I'm super optimistic actually.

00:07:45.645 --> 00:07:48.575
And it's just hard work,
education doesn't correct

00:07:48.575 --> 00:07:50.935
for a lot of social challenges

00:07:50.935 --> 00:07:52.795
but it's absolutely necessary.

00:07:52.795 --> 00:07:53.805
We have to invest in that

00:07:53.805 --> 00:07:56.975
as we think about ways of
improving social mobility

00:07:56.975 --> 00:07:59.825
we're broadly and housing
and income supports

00:07:59.825 --> 00:08:02.275
in child support in a
variety of different areas.

00:08:03.335 --> 00:08:05.135
- Absolutely.I want to double click

00:08:05.135 --> 00:08:06.495
on the common core a little bit

00:08:06.495 --> 00:08:09.355
because I'm very familiar
with the history of it

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:14.125
but it has since become somewhat
politicized over the years.

00:08:14.125 --> 00:08:16.075
Explain how the common core came about?

00:08:16.075 --> 00:08:17.675
I think it's important
for people to realize it,

00:08:17.675 --> 00:08:19.445
because there's sometimes this perception

00:08:19.445 --> 00:08:22.905
that it's this very much top
down thing that happened.

00:08:22.905 --> 00:08:25.638
What was the underlying
narrative behind it?

00:08:26.685 --> 00:08:28.285
- I can't give you those specific details,

00:08:28.285 --> 00:08:29.975
cause I was busy in schools at the time,

00:08:29.975 --> 00:08:31.735
but I was paying attention
to the newspapers

00:08:31.735 --> 00:08:34.045
and know a couple of the
actors who were involved.

00:08:34.045 --> 00:08:36.825
But really what happened
was States recognized

00:08:36.825 --> 00:08:39.705
that they needed fewer
higher and clearer standards.

00:08:39.705 --> 00:08:43.575
And so States really started
to lobby to get much more

00:08:43.575 --> 00:08:45.015
in the weeds about one

00:08:45.015 --> 00:08:47.265
what young people needed to know and do.

00:08:47.265 --> 00:08:50.455
And it was really building on
an earlier standards movement

00:08:50.455 --> 00:08:51.905
from the eighties and nineties.

00:08:51.905 --> 00:08:53.735
But really stripping it down and getting

00:08:53.735 --> 00:08:55.795
much more to basics.

00:08:55.795 --> 00:08:57.395
The States lobbied and then got

00:08:57.395 --> 00:08:59.105
the national governor's association

00:08:59.105 --> 00:09:01.825
and a variety of other
groups achieved a push

00:09:01.825 --> 00:09:04.165
for these common core
standards more broadly.

00:09:04.165 --> 00:09:05.905
They were ratified by individual States.

00:09:05.905 --> 00:09:08.695
They definitely were accelerated,

00:09:08.695 --> 00:09:11.568
by the us department of education.

00:09:11.568 --> 00:09:13.675
You know that the standards
are remarkably enduring.

00:09:13.675 --> 00:09:17.065
They become politicized,
but I think 40 to 43 States

00:09:17.065 --> 00:09:19.445
I don't have the exact
number have continued

00:09:19.445 --> 00:09:20.715
with this idea of standards.

00:09:20.715 --> 00:09:22.395
They've modified, they've tinkered them,

00:09:22.395 --> 00:09:25.205
they've made them more appropriate
to their local context.

00:09:25.205 --> 00:09:27.245
The core of the standards
movement is still there

00:09:27.245 --> 00:09:29.315
and it's incredibly powerful.

00:09:29.315 --> 00:09:31.625
Even when there's some
tinkering around the edges

00:09:31.625 --> 00:09:33.555
and driving better curriculum,

00:09:33.555 --> 00:09:35.455
better supplemental support services,

00:09:35.455 --> 00:09:38.035
and better assessments for young people.

00:09:38.035 --> 00:09:39.565
It's still a little
bit of a lightning rod,

00:09:39.565 --> 00:09:42.045
but it's really made a
big difference in quality.

00:09:42.045 --> 00:09:44.055
And now we have to push even deeper

00:09:44.055 --> 00:09:46.755
and understand what is it
that motivates young people?

00:09:46.755 --> 00:09:48.665
How do we create materials
and opportunities

00:09:48.665 --> 00:09:51.475
for them to learn and how
do we support teachers

00:09:51.475 --> 00:09:53.618
in that really complicated task?

00:09:54.575 --> 00:09:56.695
- Absolutely. I always get
this question whenever I speak

00:09:56.695 --> 00:09:58.415
at conferences or I speak to the press

00:09:58.415 --> 00:10:01.015
and I always say, look it, first of all

00:10:01.015 --> 00:10:03.575
the politicization of it
doesn't make sense to me.

00:10:03.575 --> 00:10:05.845
This was done as really a governors

00:10:05.845 --> 00:10:07.365
to your point like States by state.

00:10:07.365 --> 00:10:10.155
They said, it's no pre-Common core.

00:10:10.155 --> 00:10:11.975
The big States, New York, California

00:10:11.975 --> 00:10:14.135
and Texas had all of the power

00:10:14.135 --> 00:10:16.705
because that's where the
publishers would cater to.

00:10:16.705 --> 00:10:18.915
Then everyone else would
get some derivative version

00:10:18.915 --> 00:10:20.505
of what the publishers were creating

00:10:20.505 --> 00:10:23.575
for New York, California, and Texas.

00:10:23.575 --> 00:10:25.535
Then we also know that
in any standards creation

00:10:25.535 --> 00:10:26.795
everyone kind of wants to throw

00:10:26.795 --> 00:10:28.225
in their favorite pet project.

00:10:28.225 --> 00:10:30.755
But we realize looking at
countries like Singapore

00:10:30.755 --> 00:10:32.085
and Finland, that it's the best practice

00:10:32.085 --> 00:10:34.815
and it makes common sense
to focus on fewer things

00:10:34.815 --> 00:10:36.365
but do them with real depth.

00:10:36.365 --> 00:10:38.455
And I'd also argue that if you

00:10:38.455 --> 00:10:40.645
the correlation between
being able to do well

00:10:40.645 --> 00:10:43.565
in the common core standards
and any other strong standards,

00:10:43.565 --> 00:10:45.185
especially many of them very much derived

00:10:45.185 --> 00:10:48.345
from the common core is very high.

00:10:48.345 --> 00:10:49.715
It does feel like a little bit of a

00:10:49.715 --> 00:10:52.635
a red herring for all
of that focus on that.

00:10:52.635 --> 00:10:53.725
What your sense?

00:10:53.725 --> 00:10:55.475
You're talking about,
you have the standards

00:10:55.475 --> 00:10:57.565
and then the layer above
that is the curriculum,

00:10:57.565 --> 00:11:00.165
which is what our students
and teachers doing

00:11:00.165 --> 00:11:01.255
on a daily basis.

00:11:01.255 --> 00:11:02.475
what are their practice problems?

00:11:02.475 --> 00:11:04.655
What does the instruction look like?

00:11:04.655 --> 00:11:08.185
How much is it a curricular design issue

00:11:08.185 --> 00:11:09.815
versus the implementation

00:11:09.815 --> 00:11:12.958
of the curriculum inside of
the classroom, or is it both?

00:11:13.865 --> 00:11:15.055
- I think it's really both.

00:11:15.055 --> 00:11:16.855
What's exciting about where we've come

00:11:16.855 --> 00:11:19.155
in the last five years
is, when the foundation

00:11:19.155 --> 00:11:22.305
started doing this work, we
really invested in unit plan.

00:11:22.305 --> 00:11:24.575
The ability to create
small units that teachers

00:11:24.575 --> 00:11:26.835
would generate,and
implement in their classes.

00:11:26.835 --> 00:11:27.715
And that was powerful.

00:11:27.715 --> 00:11:30.015
There were a number of good
units that were developed.

00:11:30.015 --> 00:11:31.275
But unfortunately it meant that

00:11:31.275 --> 00:11:33.385
we were always individualizing the work.

00:11:33.385 --> 00:11:36.145
We were always creating
idiosyncratic units,

00:11:36.145 --> 00:11:37.705
that couldn't be shared across groups

00:11:37.705 --> 00:11:41.135
of teachers to really improve
their practice over time.

00:11:41.135 --> 00:11:42.965
Teaching is really
important as an individual

00:11:42.965 --> 00:11:44.135
but it's a team sport.

00:11:44.135 --> 00:11:46.355
So what we're seeing now is the emergence

00:11:46.355 --> 00:11:47.945
of a second generation of curriculum.

00:11:47.945 --> 00:11:50.915
Where not only is there a
common scope and sequence,

00:11:50.915 --> 00:11:53.875
but there's lots of teacher
choice, and the ability

00:11:53.875 --> 00:11:56.175
of teachers work to
working together to judge

00:11:56.175 --> 00:11:58.325
whether or not they're having an impact

00:11:58.325 --> 00:12:01.065
in particular classrooms
on a daily or weekly basis.

00:12:01.065 --> 00:12:03.785
I think that's the big next advance.

00:12:03.785 --> 00:12:07.105
You see places like Washington
DC doing their leap program

00:12:07.105 --> 00:12:09.335
where they, they adopted
a common curriculum.

00:12:09.335 --> 00:12:12.805
It wasn't really just to
shackle teachers and say

00:12:12.805 --> 00:12:14.145
you had to teach to the curriculum.

00:12:14.145 --> 00:12:16.475
It was actually to give
them a basis to then

00:12:16.475 --> 00:12:18.755
modify the curriculum materials

00:12:18.755 --> 00:12:21.125
against the needs of
their individual students.

00:12:21.125 --> 00:12:23.675
The variation they saw in
their classes, and work

00:12:23.675 --> 00:12:26.945
with their colleagues to almost
like Japanese lesson study.

00:12:26.945 --> 00:12:29.765
Study the lesson, implement
the study, the lesson

00:12:29.765 --> 00:12:32.295
understand which students were successful

00:12:32.295 --> 00:12:34.025
in which students failed.

00:12:34.025 --> 00:12:37.365
Then come back and re-engineer
that lesson for the next day.

00:12:37.365 --> 00:12:39.075
So it's real time improvement.

00:12:39.075 --> 00:12:40.705
It's real-time measurement.

00:12:40.705 --> 00:12:42.945
It's not a standardized test,

00:12:42.945 --> 00:12:45.735
but it's an constant
monitoring of student progress

00:12:45.735 --> 00:12:48.485
against the common core
is conceptualization

00:12:48.485 --> 00:12:49.705
of what we want students to know

00:12:49.705 --> 00:12:50.945
and be able to do.

00:12:50.945 --> 00:12:54.125
That really becomes
powerful and really exciting

00:12:54.125 --> 00:12:55.735
when you see it well done.

00:12:55.735 --> 00:12:57.515
So we're putting our foot on the gas.

00:12:57.515 --> 00:12:59.585
We've worked hard to
kind of identify a bunch

00:12:59.585 --> 00:13:02.055
of resources that are
aligned to the common core.

00:13:02.055 --> 00:13:04.745
And we're now in the process
of going deeper, figuring

00:13:04.745 --> 00:13:07.985
out what is effective for
which students when and why.

00:13:07.985 --> 00:13:09.805
And use those design principles to push

00:13:09.805 --> 00:13:13.185
for a deeper sense of quality
in the curriculum space.

00:13:13.185 --> 00:13:16.475
And then your rights Sal,
teachers are crucial.

00:13:16.475 --> 00:13:19.105
So really kind of doing embedded
professional development.

00:13:19.105 --> 00:13:21.855
So the object of study and
professional development

00:13:21.855 --> 00:13:25.145
isn't something abstract
that a supervisor develops.

00:13:25.145 --> 00:13:27.485
It's what students are
learning in your classroom.

00:13:27.485 --> 00:13:28.318
Why are they learning?

00:13:28.318 --> 00:13:29.955
Which students are not learning?

00:13:29.955 --> 00:13:32.025
Why are they failing to master a concept?

00:13:32.025 --> 00:13:34.165
Really building professional development

00:13:34.165 --> 00:13:37.095
that helps teachers solve
those problems in real time.

00:13:37.095 --> 00:13:39.295
That's what's job embedded
professional development

00:13:39.295 --> 00:13:41.445
is about and that's
what I'm excited about.

00:13:42.335 --> 00:13:44.735
We shouldn't forget students
and families in this equation.

00:13:44.735 --> 00:13:47.785
And that's where I think
Khan Academy is so crucial.

00:13:47.785 --> 00:13:49.655
The fact that young people can kind of go

00:13:49.655 --> 00:13:52.285
and get extra help,
follow scope and sequence.

00:13:52.285 --> 00:13:54.657
And the Khans platform, is really powerful

00:13:54.657 --> 00:13:57.292
and it enables them to do
the deliberative practice

00:13:57.292 --> 00:14:01.447
that reinforces the work
they're doing in classes.

00:14:01.447 --> 00:14:02.775
I think it's the mixture of what we do

00:14:02.775 --> 00:14:05.645
in class and out of class
that can really be powerful,

00:14:05.645 --> 00:14:07.988
to driving kids to
higher levels of mastery.

00:14:09.125 --> 00:14:10.085
- Yeah. And not just young people.

00:14:10.085 --> 00:14:11.985
I've heard, about 85 year
olds learning calculus

00:14:11.985 --> 00:14:12.818
on the platform.

00:14:16.241 --> 00:14:17.485
- The more the merrier I say

00:14:17.485 --> 00:14:19.335
everybody should be on that platform.

00:14:21.115 --> 00:14:21.948
- No, that absolutely.

00:14:21.948 --> 00:14:24.625
And I know, you mentioned
that you have a renewed

00:14:24.625 --> 00:14:25.885
or a double-down focus

00:14:25.885 --> 00:14:28.905
on historically
under-resourced populations.

00:14:28.905 --> 00:14:30.745
But also there's this focus on math,

00:14:30.745 --> 00:14:32.995
and I know in particular algebra one.

00:14:32.995 --> 00:14:36.835
What is it about algebra one
that, and you're preaching

00:14:36.835 --> 00:14:38.775
to the choir because whatever
you say, I'm gonna double

00:14:38.775 --> 00:14:41.435
down (laughs) on why
algebra one is so important.

00:14:41.435 --> 00:14:45.092
But in your mind why do you
see that as something of a

00:14:45.092 --> 00:14:47.198
of a really important leverage point?

00:14:48.255 --> 00:14:50.295
- Well, so I knew intellectually

00:14:50.295 --> 00:14:51.565
that algebra one was important.

00:14:51.565 --> 00:14:54.375
When you look at the data
it's, a foundational language

00:14:54.375 --> 00:14:57.085
and gateway course for
higher STEM classes.

00:14:57.085 --> 00:14:59.075
We also know it's one of
the most failed courses

00:14:59.075 --> 00:14:59.908
in high school.

00:14:59.908 --> 00:15:01.945
Students who don't succeed
in it really struggle

00:15:01.945 --> 00:15:04.305
for the remaining three
years that they're there.

00:15:04.305 --> 00:15:06.045
In some ways, it's that
pivot point where you're

00:15:06.045 --> 00:15:09.085
really not learning you're out
in your mathematics tables,

00:15:09.085 --> 00:15:11.575
but you're using a
hundred apply mathematics

00:15:11.575 --> 00:15:12.918
to solving problems.

00:15:12.918 --> 00:15:15.715
It's that first foray
into abstract reasoning.

00:15:15.715 --> 00:15:17.425
And it's so crucial, not only in math

00:15:17.425 --> 00:15:19.505
but in a variety of other subjects.

00:15:19.505 --> 00:15:21.855
But the funny part to me was I
knew all that intellectually,

00:15:21.855 --> 00:15:24.025
but we've started to gravitate towards

00:15:24.025 --> 00:15:25.425
some of the math work largely

00:15:25.425 --> 00:15:27.955
because that's where
the field wanted to go.

00:15:27.955 --> 00:15:30.765
What we started to see was
when we created the networks

00:15:30.765 --> 00:15:32.725
for school improvement for example,

00:15:32.725 --> 00:15:34.795
there was a real interest in mathematics

00:15:34.795 --> 00:15:38.145
as a key subject matter
area to getting young people

00:15:38.145 --> 00:15:39.665
on track by the end of eighth grade.

00:15:39.665 --> 00:15:42.155
Or it was a subject that
young people were struggling

00:15:42.155 --> 00:15:43.335
with in ninth grade.

00:15:43.335 --> 00:15:45.595
So we increasingly saw
a significant number

00:15:45.595 --> 00:15:49.265
of our grant tees hunkering
down on mathematics.

00:15:49.265 --> 00:15:52.555
We also saw the ability to
really build great products

00:15:52.555 --> 00:15:54.815
with grantees around
mathematics and get them

00:15:54.815 --> 00:15:57.525
out like illustrative map and a variety

00:15:57.525 --> 00:16:00.705
of the open educational
resource materials available.

00:16:00.705 --> 00:16:03.495
So we gravitated towards math largely

00:16:03.495 --> 00:16:07.595
because the field was
gravitating towards math,

00:16:07.595 --> 00:16:08.995
I'm glad they are.

00:16:08.995 --> 00:16:11.775
So many jobs are related to STEM,

00:16:11.775 --> 00:16:14.035
and so much is determined by

00:16:14.035 --> 00:16:16.515
whether a young person
can succeed in math.

00:16:16.515 --> 00:16:19.605
But I'm super excited about
now is not only thinking

00:16:19.605 --> 00:16:22.135
about algebra and all the
math that goes into it,

00:16:22.135 --> 00:16:23.295
but the emerging movement

00:16:23.295 --> 00:16:24.455
around math pathways.

00:16:24.455 --> 00:16:27.275
Really doubling down on
statistics and data science

00:16:27.275 --> 00:16:29.355
for young people, which I think

00:16:29.355 --> 00:16:33.215
will expand the opportunities
that young people have.

00:16:33.215 --> 00:16:34.715
Will make it much more relevant

00:16:34.715 --> 00:16:39.715
to the day-to-day life
in courses and in majors.

00:16:40.015 --> 00:16:43.245
In college that aren't necessarily related

00:16:43.245 --> 00:16:47.495
to statistics, to calculate
thinking algebra and thinking

00:16:47.495 --> 00:16:49.475
but really much broader.

00:16:49.475 --> 00:16:50.775
So there's lots going on in math,

00:16:50.775 --> 00:16:52.255
It's an incredibly exciting field.

00:16:52.255 --> 00:16:55.675
I was not particularly
good at math as a kid,

00:16:55.675 --> 00:16:58.215
but I'm super excited
about math as an adult.

00:16:58.215 --> 00:16:59.365
I, did take a diagnostic,

00:16:59.365 --> 00:17:02.248
I still have a little work to
do on my own algebra skills.

00:17:03.135 --> 00:17:05.045
- I'm going to start using
the word calculator more.

00:17:05.045 --> 00:17:08.662
That's a new word to me, but I'm going to,

00:17:08.662 --> 00:17:10.915
and I'll just double down
on what you're saying.

00:17:10.915 --> 00:17:13.125
You know, the stats better than I do, 70

00:17:13.125 --> 00:17:15.145
I think it's 65, 70% of all, kids who go

00:17:15.145 --> 00:17:17.985
to community college in the
US have to take remedial math.

00:17:17.985 --> 00:17:19.745
Which is essentially the
community college is saying,

00:17:19.745 --> 00:17:22.535
you're not even ready yet
to learn college algebra.

00:17:22.535 --> 00:17:26.475
College algebra is essentially,
like algebra one and a half.

00:17:26.475 --> 00:17:29.425
It's like someplace in between
algebra one and algebra two.

00:17:29.425 --> 00:17:32.215
And we also know that college algebra

00:17:32.215 --> 00:17:34.795
is one of these courses
that you have to take.

00:17:34.795 --> 00:17:37.448
If you want to pretty much
get a major in anything.

00:17:39.165 --> 00:17:40.025
Some people have debated

00:17:40.025 --> 00:17:41.385
does it have to be a requirement?

00:17:41.385 --> 00:17:43.515
But to your point which I agree with,

00:17:43.515 --> 00:17:46.085
I've met employers funders of Khan Academy

00:17:46.085 --> 00:17:47.265
who say they divide their employee.

00:17:47.265 --> 00:17:49.075
They run restaurants, and
they divide their employees

00:17:49.075 --> 00:17:50.425
into those who know algebra.

00:17:50.425 --> 00:17:52.675
And those who do not know
algebra, those who are capable

00:17:52.675 --> 00:17:55.815
of learning algebra and
the ones that know algebra.

00:17:55.815 --> 00:17:57.505
He puts them into the training programs.

00:17:57.505 --> 00:18:00.045
They become managers, they
become district managers.

00:18:00.045 --> 00:18:01.795
While those who don't
know algebra, they're

00:18:01.795 --> 00:18:05.435
they're kind of stuck doing
the non non-managerial

00:18:05.435 --> 00:18:06.958
the non career track jobs.

00:18:07.965 --> 00:18:10.165
For better, for worse the
world views algebra that way.

00:18:10.165 --> 00:18:11.645
And there's probably some good evidence

00:18:11.645 --> 00:18:12.785
to your point that it's like

00:18:12.785 --> 00:18:16.695
it is how you can show
critical abstract reasoning.

00:18:16.695 --> 00:18:19.315
Why do you think so many kids,

00:18:19.315 --> 00:18:21.365
algebra is in theory, learned as early

00:18:21.365 --> 00:18:23.375
as eighth or ninth grade.

00:18:23.375 --> 00:18:25.175
In some places starts
to be learned actually

00:18:25.175 --> 00:18:26.495
in the common core starts to be learned

00:18:26.495 --> 00:18:27.795
in sixth or seventh grade.

00:18:28.765 --> 00:18:31.695
Then kids take other
classes called geometry

00:18:31.695 --> 00:18:33.445
and algebra two, and pre-calculus

00:18:33.445 --> 00:18:35.765
which is really just more algebra.

00:18:35.765 --> 00:18:37.105
Sometimes even take stats or calculus,

00:18:37.105 --> 00:18:38.025
and then they go to college

00:18:38.025 --> 00:18:41.715
and they're told you're not
ready yet to even learn algebra.

00:18:41.715 --> 00:18:43.215
What do you think is going on?

00:18:44.125 --> 00:18:48.265
- I think we just need to
rethink how we teach algebra,

00:18:48.265 --> 00:18:50.025
and all the mathematics that lead up

00:18:50.025 --> 00:18:52.625
to algebra, and then make algebra relevant

00:18:52.625 --> 00:18:53.905
to young people in their lives.

00:18:53.905 --> 00:18:55.475
So I think it ranges

00:18:55.475 --> 00:18:58.855
from the dearth of really
high quality teachers.

00:18:58.855 --> 00:19:01.315
We've done a lousy job
preparing math teachers.

00:19:01.315 --> 00:19:02.395
We can do a better job,

00:19:02.395 --> 00:19:04.415
and we're seeing some
exemplary programs exist

00:19:04.415 --> 00:19:05.775
across the country.

00:19:05.775 --> 00:19:08.195
I think there are real challenges
in making math relevant.

00:19:08.195 --> 00:19:11.425
So we've sometimes adopted
pedagogies that don't necessarily

00:19:11.425 --> 00:19:14.345
show why we're learning a
particular math problem,

00:19:14.345 --> 00:19:16.175
why it evolved in the history

00:19:16.175 --> 00:19:18.605
of mathematics and what
problem it's solving.

00:19:18.605 --> 00:19:21.025
I think making it much more relevant just

00:19:21.025 --> 00:19:22.537
in its own history is important.

00:19:22.537 --> 00:19:25.455
What problems can you solve
in the world using this math?

00:19:25.455 --> 00:19:29.015
I remember I've sat in math
classes that were just deadly,

00:19:29.015 --> 00:19:31.275
and then I've sat in math
classes that were so exciting

00:19:31.275 --> 00:19:33.945
because the teacher was making math real

00:19:33.945 --> 00:19:34.995
in the lives of kids

00:19:34.995 --> 00:19:38.385
in the lives of communities,
in the lives of workers.

00:19:38.385 --> 00:19:40.645
What are you going to need
when you're growing up

00:19:40.645 --> 00:19:43.055
and functioning in a job.

00:19:43.055 --> 00:19:44.795
what kind of math is going to be essential

00:19:44.795 --> 00:19:46.355
to doing that job well?

00:19:46.355 --> 00:19:49.735
So I think, there's a whole
pedagogical aspect of math.

00:19:49.735 --> 00:19:52.085
And then, the new technologies really

00:19:52.085 --> 00:19:54.115
give us opportunities to try to think

00:19:54.115 --> 00:19:55.995
about math in different ways.

00:19:55.995 --> 00:19:58.585
Some people learn math by visualizing it.

00:19:58.585 --> 00:20:00.685
Other people learn math
through multiple ways

00:20:00.685 --> 00:20:03.065
of solving a problem to
get to the right answer.

00:20:03.065 --> 00:20:06.525
All of those new strategies
and techniques are powerful.

00:20:06.525 --> 00:20:09.725
So in a word creating more coherence,

00:20:09.725 --> 00:20:11.535
creating greater relevance,

00:20:11.535 --> 00:20:15.155
and creating a pedagogy that
enables young people to persist

00:20:15.155 --> 00:20:16.655
and succeed over time.

00:20:16.655 --> 00:20:18.325
I think if we can really institutionalize

00:20:18.325 --> 00:20:21.445
and scale those tools, we're
goinna see a rapid growth

00:20:21.445 --> 00:20:23.395
in math over the next five to 10 years.

00:20:24.525 --> 00:20:27.135
- And what's your sense of just students

00:20:27.135 --> 00:20:28.235
foundational knowledge?

00:20:28.235 --> 00:20:29.475
Cause to some degree when they go

00:20:29.475 --> 00:20:30.695
to college and the
constantly you're not even

00:20:30.695 --> 00:20:32.905
ready to learn algebra yet,

00:20:32.905 --> 00:20:36.315
it's almost a side effect of
by-product of year after year.

00:20:36.315 --> 00:20:38.875
Maybe the students didn't
have the foundations to go

00:20:38.875 --> 00:20:41.285
to the next course, but the
next course was being taught.

00:20:41.285 --> 00:20:42.435
And then all of a sudden it gets enforced

00:20:42.435 --> 00:20:43.268
at the college level.

00:20:43.268 --> 00:20:46.245
Which is you could imagine
an incredibly demoralizing

00:20:46.245 --> 00:20:48.315
I've visited middle school classes

00:20:48.315 --> 00:20:49.465
that shouldn't be learning algebra.

00:20:49.465 --> 00:20:52.425
Where it's clear that a
good number of the kids are,

00:20:52.425 --> 00:20:54.375
you ask them eight times seven?

00:20:54.375 --> 00:20:56.115
They know what multiplication is,

00:20:56.115 --> 00:20:58.485
but they have to recompute it.

00:20:58.485 --> 00:21:01.885
They don't immediately
jump to Oh, it's 56.

00:21:01.885 --> 00:21:04.175
And then all that computational lounge.

00:21:04.175 --> 00:21:07.475
What's the, how do you
think we can address that?

00:21:07.475 --> 00:21:09.795
This ability for students to make sure

00:21:09.795 --> 00:21:10.935
they have strong foundation.

00:21:10.935 --> 00:21:12.225
So that we're building on top of that

00:21:12.225 --> 00:21:15.395
as opposed to just promoting
them year after year,

00:21:15.395 --> 00:21:19.145
and then they eventually become
part of that 70% statistic.

00:21:19.145 --> 00:21:20.115
- It's a great question.

00:21:20.115 --> 00:21:21.655
I think one of the challenges

00:21:21.655 --> 00:21:23.535
is to understand what greater precision.

00:21:23.535 --> 00:21:27.035
What students know and are
don't know in a particular area.

00:21:27.035 --> 00:21:29.085
A foundational skills are essential.

00:21:29.085 --> 00:21:31.445
But foundational skills
and knowledge are crucial,

00:21:31.445 --> 00:21:34.555
and not fluidity in
math as defined by time.

00:21:34.555 --> 00:21:37.145
For example, one of the things
I didn't mention, but I think

00:21:37.145 --> 00:21:39.485
about a lot because of my
own personal experience

00:21:39.485 --> 00:21:42.308
in mathematics is, what turns
the person on to believe

00:21:42.308 --> 00:21:45.525
that their math person or
what alienates a person

00:21:45.525 --> 00:21:47.745
from being a math person
early on in their lives?

00:21:47.745 --> 00:21:49.865
That's true of writing
that you're reading.

00:21:49.865 --> 00:21:52.905
But in math in particular,
why is it that so

00:21:52.905 --> 00:21:55.965
many young kids, particularly
girls, particularly poor kids

00:21:55.965 --> 00:21:58.265
don't think of themselves as math people?

00:21:58.265 --> 00:22:01.455
Even though they may have the
native ability to do the work.

00:22:01.455 --> 00:22:04.485
In my own experience, I
screwed up and sixth grade,

00:22:04.485 --> 00:22:06.995
I was in a parochial
school, I was in math.

00:22:06.995 --> 00:22:09.895
I fell behind, I became
embarrassed about that.

00:22:09.895 --> 00:22:11.605
I was able to through the intervention

00:22:11.605 --> 00:22:13.425
of a really good teacher
who brought me back

00:22:13.425 --> 00:22:14.258
at the end of the year.

00:22:14.258 --> 00:22:15.775
It didn't make me feel bad,

00:22:15.775 --> 00:22:17.575
said we're going to work on this together.

00:22:17.575 --> 00:22:20.565
Paste it that individualization
that's so crucial

00:22:20.565 --> 00:22:23.225
for many young people in education.

00:22:23.225 --> 00:22:25.355
She did that for a number of years

00:22:25.355 --> 00:22:28.025
and was able to kind
of really bring me back

00:22:28.025 --> 00:22:30.285
in a six month period to
be back on grade level.

00:22:30.285 --> 00:22:33.065
I was lucky, I think lots of young people

00:22:33.065 --> 00:22:35.375
that I've spoken with, don't have teachers

00:22:35.375 --> 00:22:37.295
like that who have the time or resource

00:22:37.295 --> 00:22:41.345
and facility, to be able to
give the individualization.

00:22:41.345 --> 00:22:42.815
If I hadn't gotten that,

00:22:42.815 --> 00:22:45.860
I sure I would have been
alienated from math.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:47.225
That disenfranchised for math.

00:22:47.225 --> 00:22:48.895
Not believing I was good at math

00:22:48.895 --> 00:22:51.775
and then slowly getting
alienated and not motivated.

00:22:51.775 --> 00:22:54.055
So I think it's foundational skills.

00:22:54.055 --> 00:22:55.665
And I think it's the
way we emphasize those

00:22:55.665 --> 00:22:58.305
foundational skills to
build Napa identity,

00:22:58.305 --> 00:23:00.967
and enthusiasm for math.

00:23:00.967 --> 00:23:03.245
It's not only being able to do math,

00:23:03.245 --> 00:23:04.455
but I hope we can get to a place

00:23:04.455 --> 00:23:05.815
where kids actually enjoy math.

00:23:05.815 --> 00:23:07.855
Because it's an incredibly elegant

00:23:07.855 --> 00:23:10.505
and beautiful way of
representing the universe.

00:23:10.505 --> 00:23:13.465
I once had a math teacher who
said a beautiful problem is

00:23:13.465 --> 00:23:15.065
like a beautiful poem.

00:23:15.065 --> 00:23:16.725
I don't think can find that far,

00:23:16.725 --> 00:23:18.165
I'm much more a poetry person

00:23:18.165 --> 00:23:20.105
than a beautiful problem in math person.

00:23:20.105 --> 00:23:21.842
But I think there's a real
grain of truth in that.

00:23:21.842 --> 00:23:25.095
And the more we can excite
students math in that way,

00:23:25.095 --> 00:23:27.795
the more likely we're
able to be, we'll see

00:23:27.795 --> 00:23:29.245
gains in student achievement.

00:23:30.505 --> 00:23:31.745
- Yeah. Well I agree.

00:23:31.745 --> 00:23:35.355
I love poetry too, but
sometimes when you see a

00:23:35.355 --> 00:23:37.675
beautiful product in math or a beautiful

00:23:37.675 --> 00:23:39.695
it says something very
deep about the universe.

00:23:39.695 --> 00:23:42.015
That's whats amazing, the
math that you're doing

00:23:42.015 --> 00:23:45.415
is just abstract symbols
but it's representing

00:23:45.415 --> 00:23:48.505
a very pure idea that I think sometimes

00:23:48.505 --> 00:23:49.885
can give you chills.

00:23:49.885 --> 00:23:53.825
I mean I can start kicking
out on all of those things.

00:23:53.825 --> 00:23:55.235
There's a lot of talk right now

00:23:55.235 --> 00:23:58.315
because of the pandemic learning loss kids

00:23:58.315 --> 00:23:59.685
who've been disengaged.

00:23:59.685 --> 00:24:02.375
I'm curious about what y'all
have been seeing on that front.

00:24:02.375 --> 00:24:06.765
But also in this last a bill
coming through Congress, 120

00:24:06.765 --> 00:24:11.215
I think $6 billion is
going down to the States

00:24:11.215 --> 00:24:13.305
which will then go to the
districts in the schools.

00:24:13.305 --> 00:24:18.185
20% of which has been kind
of tagged for learning loss.

00:24:18.185 --> 00:24:20.625
This is a lot of money that is flowing

00:24:20.625 --> 00:24:23.025
into the States into school systems.

00:24:23.025 --> 00:24:24.515
What's your hopes and fears there?

00:24:24.515 --> 00:24:26.775
What do you think this can
be a long-term accelerant?

00:24:26.775 --> 00:24:28.245
Cause I don't think there's
ever been an example

00:24:28.245 --> 00:24:31.615
of this much money flowing
to schools this quickly.

00:24:31.615 --> 00:24:32.725
- Well, just to give you an example

00:24:32.725 --> 00:24:34.665
we spend $300 million a year

00:24:34.665 --> 00:24:36.955
at the Gates foundation
on public education.

00:24:36.955 --> 00:24:41.225
Schools are about to
receive $120 billion a year.

00:24:41.225 --> 00:24:43.815
So that really dwarves
everything that's occurring

00:24:43.815 --> 00:24:45.155
in philanthropy.

00:24:45.155 --> 00:24:46.615
So one of the things we've been working

00:24:46.615 --> 00:24:47.965
on is to really try to think

00:24:47.965 --> 00:24:50.775
about how to create
coherence and coordination.

00:24:50.775 --> 00:24:53.625
So people don't just spend
money on the best idea

00:24:53.625 --> 00:24:56.145
that they've thought but
they really are connected

00:24:56.145 --> 00:24:57.995
to the research and
thinking hard about what

00:24:57.995 --> 00:25:01.565
are the elements that students
need to be successful.

00:25:01.565 --> 00:25:03.695
And so over the last few
weeks we've been working

00:25:03.695 --> 00:25:05.495
with three really outstanding groups

00:25:06.495 --> 00:25:09.645
council of chief state school
officers, chiefs for change

00:25:09.645 --> 00:25:11.435
and the council of great city schools.

00:25:11.435 --> 00:25:14.215
To really build out a 10 point framework,

00:25:14.215 --> 00:25:15.965
key elements that need to be addressed

00:25:15.965 --> 00:25:17.125
in any recovery effort.

00:25:17.125 --> 00:25:19.915
And then really start to
disseminate that and figure

00:25:19.915 --> 00:25:23.642
out what are the best practices,
best organizations and

00:25:23.642 --> 00:25:27.155
and tools and resources
that can be implemented

00:25:27.155 --> 00:25:28.385
in each one of those areas.

00:25:28.385 --> 00:25:33.025
So concretely we know that
much of the learning loss need

00:25:33.989 --> 00:25:36.845
for acceleration has been
created by the digital divide.

00:25:36.845 --> 00:25:39.685
We still have eight to
12 million students who

00:25:39.685 --> 00:25:41.775
don't have regular access
either to equipment

00:25:41.775 --> 00:25:44.545
or access to the internet.

00:25:44.545 --> 00:25:45.985
We need to solve that problem.

00:25:45.985 --> 00:25:49.085
And hopefully 7 billion of
the dollars that are going

00:25:49.085 --> 00:25:51.565
through that stimulus
package are designed to go

00:25:51.565 --> 00:25:53.825
through the FCC and start to address it.

00:25:53.825 --> 00:25:55.995
We're gonna have to continue
to work with districts

00:25:55.995 --> 00:25:59.825
and States that both solve
the problem in the short-term

00:25:59.825 --> 00:26:01.375
but then in the long-term.

00:26:01.375 --> 00:26:03.115
But then, what are the strategies

00:26:03.115 --> 00:26:04.695
that are research-based
that can be helpful?

00:26:04.695 --> 00:26:06.395
Curriculum is really important.

00:26:06.395 --> 00:26:09.595
End of your courses is really
important, opportunities to go

00:26:09.595 --> 00:26:12.185
on Khan Academy and do the extra homework

00:26:12.185 --> 00:26:13.595
is really important.

00:26:13.595 --> 00:26:16.265
But they're also just
individual needs like tutoring.

00:26:16.265 --> 00:26:18.145
We've been lucky to work with a variety

00:26:18.145 --> 00:26:21.275
of different tutoring groups
that have seen real advances

00:26:21.275 --> 00:26:25.155
of acceleration one to two
years in a year of tutoring.

00:26:25.155 --> 00:26:29.815
So it'll really be about
identifying those best practices

00:26:29.815 --> 00:26:32.605
in coordinating I'm sure like you,

00:26:32.605 --> 00:26:34.125
I really have the opportunity to talk

00:26:34.125 --> 00:26:36.725
to a lot of superintendents
and chiefs around the country.

00:26:36.725 --> 00:26:38.925
It's one of the joys of my job.

00:26:38.925 --> 00:26:40.785
And I have to say I've been blown

00:26:40.785 --> 00:26:43.045
away by how thoughtful people are.

00:26:43.045 --> 00:26:44.625
Absorbing this amount of money in ways

00:26:44.625 --> 00:26:46.595
that are, is sustainable over time

00:26:46.595 --> 00:26:49.325
and really enables us
to build back better,

00:26:49.325 --> 00:26:52.212
but built back braver
is going to be crucial.

00:26:52.212 --> 00:26:54.895
And I think in the next
two or three months

00:26:54.895 --> 00:26:56.845
we're gonna see some really
interesting plans coming

00:26:56.845 --> 00:26:59.135
from a variety of different districts

00:26:59.135 --> 00:27:00.955
addressing learning
loss, addressing social

00:27:00.955 --> 00:27:02.685
emotional needs, thinking hard

00:27:02.685 --> 00:27:05.635
about the digital divide,
thinking hard about,

00:27:05.635 --> 00:27:07.175
the host of things that need to happen to

00:27:07.175 --> 00:27:09.325
enable our young people
to recover quickly.

00:27:10.275 --> 00:27:11.108
- Completely agree.

00:27:11.108 --> 00:27:13.105
I've actually just actually
last Friday started

00:27:13.105 --> 00:27:15.095
cause cause I talked to so many of these

00:27:15.095 --> 00:27:16.405
superintendents of large districts

00:27:16.405 --> 00:27:18.101
chief academic officers,
state commissioners

00:27:18.101 --> 00:27:20.485
or state superintendents, and they are all

00:27:20.485 --> 00:27:23.786
both excited and fearful (laughs)
of what's about to happen.

00:27:23.786 --> 00:27:26.655
Because there's a huge
opportunity to do a lot of good

00:27:26.655 --> 00:27:28.425
but there's a lot of political pressure

00:27:28.425 --> 00:27:31.175
to put the money to work fast
but also not to squander.

00:27:31.175 --> 00:27:34.165
Which is reasonable political
pressure on both fronts.

00:27:34.165 --> 00:27:35.595
Because of, but I've been very impressed

00:27:35.595 --> 00:27:38.005
with how people have
been thinking about it.

00:27:38.005 --> 00:27:39.825
How do you ramp into and it's over roughly

00:27:39.825 --> 00:27:42.085
the next three, three years or so.

00:27:42.085 --> 00:27:43.225
And how do you not make it?

00:27:43.225 --> 00:27:46.495
So it's just this blip and
then it doesn't sustain itself.

00:27:46.495 --> 00:27:49.665
How do you use these resources
to do real structural change?

00:27:49.665 --> 00:27:51.775
And obviously I've had my bigger hat

00:27:51.775 --> 00:27:55.095
the Khan Academy hat,
which plays a role where

00:27:55.095 --> 00:27:57.115
kids can asynchronously
get practice feedback

00:27:57.115 --> 00:27:58.215
and work in a classroom.

00:27:58.215 --> 00:28:00.095
And then this new effort
around schoolhouse.

00:28:00.095 --> 00:28:01.125
World versus around tutoring,

00:28:01.125 --> 00:28:03.625
which is exactly why
this focus on tutoring.

00:28:03.625 --> 00:28:05.975
Cause it's great to be able
to go to a Khan Academy,

00:28:05.975 --> 00:28:08.575
but unless you have folks
to mentor you, coach you

00:28:08.575 --> 00:28:10.416
you have a community to be in.

00:28:10.416 --> 00:28:12.395
It's a lot harder for a lot of students

00:28:12.395 --> 00:28:13.755
to be able to engage at that level.

00:28:13.755 --> 00:28:15.205
So yeah, it's gonna be interesting to

00:28:15.205 --> 00:28:17.125
see how all of this evolves.

00:28:17.125 --> 00:28:20.725
I want to make sure I get
to questions from folks.

00:28:20.725 --> 00:28:23.725
Let's see, I've asked some
of these well, I'll ask one

00:28:23.725 --> 00:28:24.895
which is kind of a fun question.

00:28:24.895 --> 00:28:26.455
It's a less deep policy question

00:28:26.455 --> 00:28:28.155
but I think you have a
unique vantage point.

00:28:28.155 --> 00:28:31.005
This is from SmartBear on YouTube.

00:28:31.005 --> 00:28:33.345
What does it feel like
working with Bill and Melinda?

00:28:33.345 --> 00:28:34.308
What are they like?

00:28:35.175 --> 00:28:36.585
- I wonder Bill and Melinda like,

00:28:36.585 --> 00:28:38.608
Bill and Melinda are fantastic.

00:28:40.165 --> 00:28:43.320
I've been in meetings with
you saw, that Bill is one

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:45.815
of the smartest people
you'll ever deal with.

00:28:45.815 --> 00:28:49.695
He really wants to ask the
hard questions, kick the tires.

00:28:49.695 --> 00:28:50.925
He has a point of view.

00:28:50.925 --> 00:28:52.555
He tells you what that point of view is.

00:28:52.555 --> 00:28:54.595
And he really expects
you to tell your point

00:28:54.595 --> 00:28:57.505
of view back and not
be intimidated by him.

00:28:57.505 --> 00:28:59.945
And we've had just amazing conversations.

00:28:59.945 --> 00:29:01.225
I never walk into the room.

00:29:01.225 --> 00:29:02.785
I feel like I know a lot about education

00:29:02.785 --> 00:29:05.475
